\title[Cubic pinning of a non-abelian fork]{Cubic Pinning of a Non-Abelian Fork:\\
Congruence Information in the $A_4$-Field of $x^4+8x+12$}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 14, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study how much information a congruence condition on a prime $p$ can carry about the splitting type of a fixed polynomial modulo $p$, and we settle the structural question of what determines that amount. Measuring a binary splitting observable (a \emph{fork}) against a residue observable (a \emph{dial}) with Shannon mutual information, we prove a \emph{trichotomy}: $0 \le I \le H(\bar f)$, with $I=0$ exactly for conditionally constant (\emph{flat}) forks, $I = H(\bar f)$ exactly for forks that are deterministic functions of the dial (\emph{pinned}), and $0<I<H(\bar f)$ otherwise (\emph{leaking}). We prove a \emph{pinning-content criterion}: a fork is pinnable by a congruence exactly when it factors through the abelianization $G^{\ab}$ of the Galois group, equivalently when it is invariant under translation by commutators.

The main witness is $A_4$, realized as the Galois group of $x^4+8x+12$. We prove $[A_4,A_4]=V_4$ and $|A_4^{\ab}|=3$, compute the Klein resolvent $y^3-48y-64$, verify $\disc = 576^2$ for both quartic and resolvent, and identify the resolvent, via $y=4z$ giving $64(z^3-3z-1)$, with the conductor-$9$ cyclic cubic $\Q(\zeta_9)^+$. Hence the fork $F_0(p)=[\Frob p \in V_4]$, equivalently $p \equiv \pm 1 \pmod 9$, is pinned with
\[ I(p \bmod 9;\ F_0) = H(1/3) = \log_2 3 - \tfrac23 = 0.9183\ldots \text{ bits},\]
the first cubic pinning of a non-abelian field, and numerically identical to the abelian cyclic cubic case. The modulus $9$ is minimal and coprime moduli are flat. We prove \emph{within-$V_4$ flatness} --- every homomorphism from $A_4$ to an abelian group is trivial on $V_4$ --- so the finer fork $F_1(p)=[\Frob p = e]$ is unpinnable by any modulus, yet leaks exactly $H(1/12)-\tfrac13 H(1/4)=0.1434\ldots$ bits by an exact leakage law $I = H(pq)-p\,H(q)$. At composite level we compute the AND, OR, XOR and split-count channels of a semiprime exactly, prove a which-factor wall ($I=0$ exactly), and give the $k$-factor law $H(3^{-(k+1)})-\tfrac13H(3^{-k}) \to 0$. Finally $A_5$ is perfect and hence absolutely unpinnable, closing the table $C_2/C_3/S_3/S_4/A_4/A_5$.
\end{abstract}

